 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the penal law, in relation to establishing the crime of assault on the operator of a for-hire vehicle",
    "basePrintNo": "S7022",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7022-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7022\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to establishing the crime\nof assault on the operator of a for-hire vehicle\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo protect taxi drivers from being assaulted on the job by increasing\ncriminal penalties for such assaults to those currently imposed for\nattacks on mass transit workers, and posting warnings in taxi cabs\nabout such penalties.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 titles the bill the \"taxi driver protection act.\"\n\nSection 2 amends section 120,05 of the penal by adding a new\nsubdivision 13 to define an attack on the operator of a taxi by an\nindividual whose intent is to cause physical injury as assault in the\nsecond degree.\n\nSection 3 amends subdivision 1 of section 60.07 of the penal law by\nadding language to distinguish indeterminate sentences from a\ndeterminate sentence which shall not be less than three years greater\nthan the minimum term of a determinate sentence, and paragraph b of\nsubdivision 2 of section 60.07 of the penal law to specify that a\n\"for-hire vehicle\" shall mean a vehicle designed to carry not more\nthan nine passengers. Also a new subdivision 3 is added to section\n60.07 of the penal law to require each for-hire vehicle to post a sign\ninside their vehicle warning that \"assaulting a taxi driver is felony\npunishable by up to seven years in prison.\"\n\nSection 4 provides for the act to take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nAccording to an OSHA study from 2000, taxi workers are sixty times\nmore likely to be killed on the job than other workers.  New York City\nis home to the largest taxi driver industry in the country.\n\nDefining attacks on taxi drivers as a separate offense would\nunderscore the gravity of the crime. It would likely lead to more\nprosecutions for offenses carrying enhanced sentences, as prosecutors\nwould only need to know that the elements of the crime were present,\nand not the details of a complicated sentencing provision. And it\nwould facilitate a public information campaign designed to inform the\npublic that attacks on taxi drivers are a very serious offense.\n\nIt would also bring consistency to the Penal Law by providing the same\ntreatment for crimes against taxi drivers as for crimes against other\npublic transportation workers, and would bring New York in line with\nCalifornia, District of Columbia, Illinois, and Pennsylvania, which\nalso make assaulting a taxi driver a distinct criminal offense.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Legislation.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
