    "lawCode": "Amd §§60.07 & 10.00, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7022A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to the crime of assault on\nthe operator of a for-hire vehicle\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo protect taxi drivers from being assaulted on the job by increasing\ncriminal penalties for such assaults and posting warnings in taxi cabs\nabout such penalties.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 titles the bill the \"taxi driver protection act.\"\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 1 and paragraph (a) of subdivision 2 of\nsection 60.07 of the penal law, and adds a new subdivision 3, to (1) add\nassault in the second and third degrees as specified offenses subject to\nthe related sentencing enhancement in this section, (2) limiting the\nsentence for misdemeanor offenses under this chapter to twice the maxi-\nmum definite sentence otherwise authorized, and (3) require each for-\nhire vehicle to post a sign inside their vehicle warning that \"assault-\ning a taxi driver is punishable by up to twenty-five years in prison.\"\n\nSection 3 amends section 10.00 of the penal law to add the phrase\n\"except as may otherwise be provided by law\" to the definition of\n\"misdemeanor.\n\nSection 4 provides for the act to take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nAccording to the federal Occupational Safety and Health Administration,\ntaxi workers are thirty times more likely to be killed on the job than\nother workers. New York City is home to the largest taxi driver industry\nin the country.\n\nIncreasing penalties for attacks on taxi drivers underscores the gravity\nof the crime and, together with the requirement that a warning be posted\nabout the serious criminal penalties for assaulting a taxi driver, will\nhelp to deter such crimes from occurring in the future.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Legislation.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
